{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVbiWAEa7kHKdkX2h2ez","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XUs1haypE4TLgjvBuBmNRD","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -n -i -E \"contribution|implement|transfer|sufficient statistic|p\\\\\\\\times p|comparator runs|episode|LLM usage|Artifacts|research agent|seconds|~s\\b| s,|15\\.99\" paper.tex","description":"Find lines referenced by audit findings"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3447,"cache_read_input_tokens":44497,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3447},"output_tokens":36,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e6af20b8-3edd-4696-ac58-f407db387098","uuid":"48aa80e9-4e79-43b3-8f6b-9929c1a7758f","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:41:48.708Z","request_id":"req_011CgVbiVa27nqNVv3xCSeYP"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XUs1haypE4TLgjvBuBmNRD","type":"tool_result","content":"39:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n56:\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n79:Chatbot Arena~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA} and MT-Bench~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW} established crowdsourced and judge-based pairwise evaluation of chat models. Automatic judges have known biases, such as a preference for length, which can be partly corrected~\\citep{Dubois2024LengthControlledAA}. \\citet{Min2025ImprovingYM} show that a model's Arena ranking can be improved by rigging votes, and \\citet{Singh2025TheLI} document how private testing and unequal data access distort the Arena leaderboard. \\citet{Huang2025DroppingJA} report that dropping a handful of preferences can change top LLM rankings. Adversarial attacks on rank aggregation have also been analyzed as games between attacker and aggregator~\\citep{Ma2022ATO}. On the estimation side, \\citet{Patil2020RankAF} study ranking recovery under BTL-type models when an adversary corrupts a fraction of the pairwise comparisons, which is the setting our flip perturbation instantiates on fixed real data. We do not claim to be the first to show that an Arena leader is fragile. Our contribution is the controlled comparison across crowdsourced, expert, LLM-judge, and sports designs under one estimator and protocol. We also contrast drop and flip perturbations and top-1 and top-$k$ targets, and we show that cross-design conclusions depend on the size scale.\n90:where $\\sigma$ is the logistic function and ties count as half a win for each side. The ridge weight $\\lambda$ and the tolerance $\\tau$ below are passed to the finder by the evaluation harness with each request; the harness specifies $\\lambda=0.1$ and $\\tau=10^{-6}$. The ridge term fixes the location of $\\theta$ and keeps the fit finite even when the perturbation removes all of a player's losses. According to the harness's stated protocol, players with fewer than 50 comparisons are removed iteratively before fitting. The player set is then fixed: a player keeps its index even if every one of its rows is dropped. Equation~\\eqref{eq:bt} depends on the data only through the $p\\times p$ matrices of (half-)wins $W_{ij}$ and counts $N_{ij}$. A refit after any perturbation therefore costs $O(p^3)$ per Newton step, independent of $n$. The finder's refit implements exactly this objective (source locations in Appendix~\\ref{app:repro}).\n102:The study has two components. The \\emph{finder}, which constructs perturbation sets, is released in full, and every statement we make about it can be checked against its source. The \\emph{evaluation harness}, which builds the cases, re-verifies each returned set with its own refit, runs the comparators and controls, and computes all reported scores and fractions, is not part of the artifacts accompanying this paper. Its numerical outputs are our evidence. Its internal settings (the player filter, tie mapping, comparator and control construction, data splitting, and scoring rules) are described below as the harness's \\emph{stated protocol}. They could not be checked against source here.\n117:The linear approximation only orders rows; it never sets the reported size. For a prefix $D_s$ of length $s$ we refit \\eqref{eq:bt} exactly. The refit is a damped Newton method on the perturbed sufficient statistics, warm-started at $\\hat\\theta$ and stopped once the largest absolute gradient entry falls below $10^{-10}$, and the target condition is tested on the result. The search doubles $s=1,2,4,\\dots$ until a prefix changes the target, then bisects between the last failing and the first succeeding length. This requires $O(\\log \\kver)$ refits. The search returns the shortest changing prefix it locates, and the harness re-verifies the returned set with its own independent refit. Because the refit after a perturbation is not guaranteed to be monotone in $s$, bisection may miss a shorter changing prefix. Even then, the returned set is a valid certificate. The finder is a single NumPy module (Appendix~\\ref{app:repro}). It is deterministic: its seed only initializes a random generator that the search never uses.\n126:These are also part of the harness's stated protocol. \\emph{Planted minimum:} a synthetic three-player case with a near-tied leader pair. The harness computes its exact minimal drop size by brute-force enumeration over per-pair winner counts, which are sufficient statistics for \\eqref{eq:bt}. \\emph{Row permutation:} the Arena drop/top-1 case with its rows presented in a different order. A correct instrument must return the same size. \\emph{Seed replay:} each case runs under three seeds, and one episode is replayed and its output hash compared. Before any search, the harness is stated to check each case. It rejects cases with a degenerate decisive margin ($m\\le 10\\tau$). It also requires that the empty perturbation leaves the target unchanged and that a refit on reversed row order reproduces $\\hat\\theta$.\n129:Table~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the cases. The four designs are crowdsourced votes from the Chatbot Arena 140k human-preference release~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}; expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments of MT-Bench answers~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}; and NBA games from two seasons of the FiveThirtyEight Elo record, the 2014 and 2015 seasons in that file's labeling, with each game a comparison between two teams, following the long use of BT models for sports tournaments~\\citep{Cattelan2013DynamicBM}. We fit a static BT model to each season, without the time-varying strengths such models can include. Under the harness's stated protocol, Arena ``tie'' and ``both bad'' votes and MT-Bench tie judgments become ties ($y=\\tfrac12$); NBA games have no ties. Each design contributes three cases: drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$. With the two controls this gives 17 cases. Method choices were made on separate development data, stated by the harness to be the Arena 55k release and the NBA 1983 season. The harness is also stated to exclude final rows whose content hash appears in the development data. Neither the split nor its overlap can be inspected in our artifacts. The 17 cases were then run once in a sealed evaluation under seeds 101, 102, and 103. Every episode either returns a set that the harness verifies, or counts as a failure; none failed. The evaluation completed and passed all of its validity gates.\n130:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.failed_episode_fraction]]\n161:Table~\\ref{tab:validity} collects the evidence that the measured sizes mean what we claim. On the planted synthetic case, the verified drop size is 8 and the control score is 0. The harness defines this score as $\\min(0,\\log_2(k^\\star/\\kver))$ with $k^\\star$ its enumerated minimum. Under that stated definition, a value of 0 means the instrument returned the brute-force minimum, so here, at least, the upper bound is tight. Under row permutation, the Arena drop/top-1 size is 62 in both presentations (control score 0), so the result does not depend on row order. All 15 real-case sizes are identical across seeds 101, 102, and 103. The replayed episode reproduced its output hash (replay match 1.0), and no episode failed. Since the finder is deterministic, seed agreement shows reproducibility. It does \\emph{not} measure sampling uncertainty (\\S\\ref{sec:limitations}).\n191:Replay of one episode & match $=1.0$ & identical output hash \\\\\n192:Failed episodes & fraction $0.0$ & every proposal verified \\\\\n253:For one fixed and widely used estimator, the following holds across four evaluation designs. Explicit, verified sets of tens of comparisons change the leader. Typical random perturbations need roughly 16 times as many rows. The relative fragility of designs depends on whether size is counted or proportioned, and on whether the conclusion is about the leader or a top-$k$ set. The protocol is cheap. All 17 cases, including the 135,622-row Arena case, completed in about 16\\,s of recorded episode time per seed (15.5--16.3\\,s). This covers data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit, and it is possible because each refit acts on $p\\times p$ sufficient statistics. For practitioners, this suggests reporting a verified drop and flip size alongside resampling intervals, for both the leader and any advertised top-$k$ claim.\n254:% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#episode_durations_seconds]]\n269:The direct heuristic is reported only at family level, so per-case heuristic sizes are unknown. More generally, comparator, control, and scoring details come from the harness's stated protocol, whose source is not among our artifacts. We evaluated one search configuration, the influence ordering with verified prefix search. Three refined variants were designed but not scored, so whether they find materially smaller sets remains open. Our sizes are thus conservative descriptions of fragility, not tight ones.\n290:Table~\\ref{tab:percase} lists every case of the sealed evaluation. Each size is the same under seeds 101, 102, and 103. The recorded episode time per seed, summed over all 17 cases, was 15.99\\,s, 15.55\\,s, and 16.33\\,s. This includes data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit.\n291:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n292:% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#episode_durations_seconds]]\n329:The evaluation record reports, for each family, the top-1 drop and flip fractions $\\kver/n$, averaged over seeds and, for NBA, over the two seasons. For Arena and the two MT-Bench sources, $n=\\kver_{\\text{drop}}/(\\kver/n)_{\\text{drop}}$; this yields 135,622, 3,355, and 2,400 to within floating-point error. For NBA, the season-mean drop fraction $\\tfrac12(7/n_{14}+16/n_{15})=0.8756\\%$ and flip fraction $\\tfrac12(4/n_{14}+8/n_{15})=0.4567\\%$ form a $2\\times2$ linear system in $(1/n_{14},1/n_{15})$. Its solution gives $n_{14}=1{,}319$ and $n_{15}=1{,}311$. The two seasons sum to 2,630 games; we could not check this against the harness's season file, which is not among our artifacts. The script \\nolinkurl{analysis/derive_stats.py} performs this derivation from \\texttt{paper\\_data.json} and writes \\nolinkurl{analysis/derived_stats.json}.\n336:This subsection records the evaluation harness's stated protocol. The harness source is not among the artifacts accompanying this paper, so these details are reported as specified and could not be inspected. Only the statements about what the finder receives are confirmed by the finder's own interface documentation (\\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 1--46).\n360:Three refined search configurations were designed to target that improvement. As recorded, they are relinearized category-knapsack variants: one with multi-pair targeting and pruning, one with swap refinement, and one with pruning seeded from the baseline influence set. No source for these configurations is among our artifacts, so we give no further implementation detail. All three were unscored (0 of 3 scored), and the evaluation evidence does not record why. Their effect on $\\kver$ is unknown and should not be read as a loss. No result in the main text depends on them.\n368:All data are public. The snapshots below are those listed in the task's data specification; that the harness loaded exactly these files, and the split it applied, could not be verified from our artifacts. Arena: \\nolinkurl{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-140k} at commit \\texttt{6322995ab34d}, CC-BY-4.0. MT-Bench: \\nolinkurl{lmsys/mt_bench_human_judgments} at commit \\texttt{f7d2896d2cc5}, human and GPT-4-pair files, CC-BY-4.0. NBA: \\nolinkurl{fivethirtyeight/data} at commit \\texttt{4c1ff5e3aef1}, file \\nolinkurl{nba-elo/nbaallelo.csv}, CC-BY-4.0. Development used the Arena 55k release (commit \\texttt{18c298340948}) and the NBA 1983 season.\n371:Python, CPU only; the finder depends only on NumPy and the standard library. The full sealed evaluation runs the 17 cases under three seeds plus one replay. Recorded episode time was about 16\\,s per seed; the evaluator's comparator runs add to this.\n373:\\paragraph{Artifacts.}\n376:\\subsection{LLM usage}\n379:Large language models were used extensively and are disclosed here. An autonomous LLM-based research agent proposed the study design within the task's constraints. It implemented the perturbation finder and the refined configurations, wrote the analysis and plotting scripts, and drafted this manuscript, including its framing and text. Every number in the paper comes from the sealed evaluation record produced by executing the code, and the derivation scripts are included. Bibliographic metadata was retrieved from the Semantic Scholar API. Huang et al.~(2025) is cited by title only, as part of the provided background reading list. No result or method in this paper is taken from it. No paid API or GPU was used for the experiments.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e6af20b8-3edd-4696-ac58-f407db387098","uuid":"30dd081d-0251-4dc8-ab64-6986055f289c","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:41:48.831Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"39:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n56:\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n79:Chatbot Arena~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA} and MT-Bench~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW} established crowdsourced and judge-based pairwise evaluation of chat models. Automatic judges have known biases, such as a preference for length, which can be partly corrected~\\citep{Dubois2024LengthControlledAA}. \\citet{Min2025ImprovingYM} show that a model's Arena ranking can be improved by rigging votes, and \\citet{Singh2025TheLI} document how private testing and unequal data access distort the Arena leaderboard. \\citet{Huang2025DroppingJA} report that dropping a handful of preferences can change top LLM rankings. Adversarial attacks on rank aggregation have also been analyzed as games between attacker and aggregator~\\citep{Ma2022ATO}. On the estimation side, \\citet{Patil2020RankAF} study ranking recovery under BTL-type models when an adversary corrupts a fraction of the pairwise comparisons, which is the setting our flip perturbation instantiates on fixed real data. We do not claim to be the first to show that an Arena leader is fragile. Our contribution is the controlled comparison across crowdsourced, expert, LLM-judge, and sports designs under one estimator and protocol. We also contrast drop and flip perturbations and top-1 and top-$k$ targets, and we show that cross-design conclusions depend on the size scale.\n90:where $\\sigma$ is the logistic function and ties count as half a win for each side. The ridge weight $\\lambda$ and the tolerance $\\tau$ below are passed to the finder by the evaluation harness with each request; the harness specifies $\\lambda=0.1$ and $\\tau=10^{-6}$. The ridge term fixes the location of $\\theta$ and keeps the fit finite even when the perturbation removes all of a player's losses. According to the harness's stated protocol, players with fewer than 50 comparisons are removed iteratively before fitting. The player set is then fixed: a player keeps its index even if every one of its rows is dropped. Equation~\\eqref{eq:bt} depends on the data only through the $p\\times p$ matrices of (half-)wins $W_{ij}$ and counts $N_{ij}$. A refit after any perturbation therefore costs $O(p^3)$ per Newton step, independent of $n$. The finder's refit implements exactly this objective (source locations in Appendix~\\ref{app:repro}).\n102:The study has two components. The \\emph{finder}, which constructs perturbation sets, is released in full, and every statement we make about it can be checked against its source. The \\emph{evaluation harness}, which builds the cases, re-verifies each 