control score is 0. The harness defines this score as $\\min(0,\\log_2(k^\\star/\\kver))$ with $k^\\star$ its enumerated minimum. Under that stated definition, a value of 0 means the instrument returned the brute-force minimum, so here, at least, the upper bound is tight. Under row permutation, the Arena drop/top-1 size is 62 in both presentations (control score 0), so the result does not depend on row order. All 15 real-case sizes are identical across seeds 101, 102, and 103. The replayed episode reproduced its output hash (replay match 1.0), and no episode failed. Since the finder is deterministic, seed agreement shows reproducibility. It does \\emph{not} measure sampling uncertainty (\\S\\ref{sec:limitations}).\n191:Replay of one episode & match $=1.0$ & identical output hash \\\\\n192:Failed episodes & fraction $0.0$ & every proposal verified \\\\\n253:For one fixed and widely used estimator, the following holds across four evaluation designs. Explicit, verified sets of tens of comparisons change the leader. Typical random perturbations need roughly 16 times as many rows. The relative fragility of designs depends on whether size is counted or proportioned, and on whether the conclusion is about the leader or a top-$k$ set. The protocol is cheap. All 17 cases, including the 135,622-row Arena case, completed in about 16\\,s of recorded episode time per seed (15.5--16.3\\,s). This covers data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit, and it is possible because each refit acts on $p\\times p$ sufficient statistics. For practitioners, this suggests reporting a verified drop and flip size alongside resampling intervals, for both the leader and any advertised top-$k$ claim.\n254:% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#episode_durations_seconds]]\n269:The direct heuristic is reported only at family level, so per-case heuristic sizes are unknown. More generally, comparator, control, and scoring details come from the harness's stated protocol, whose source is not among our artifacts. We evaluated one search configuration, the influence ordering with verified prefix search. Three refined variants were designed but not scored, so whether they find materially smaller sets remains open. Our sizes are thus conservative descriptions of fragility, not tight ones.\n290:Table~\\ref{tab:percase} lists every case of the sealed evaluation. Each size is the same under seeds 101, 102, and 103. The recorded episode time per seed, summed over all 17 cases, was 15.99\\,s, 15.55\\,s, and 16.33\\,s. This includes data transfer, the search, and the evaluator's verification refit.\n291:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n292:% @evidence[[analysis/derived_stats.json#episode_durations_seconds]]\n329:The evaluation record reports, for each family, the top-1 drop and flip fractions $\\kver/n$, averaged over seeds and, for NBA, over the two seasons. For Arena and the two MT-Bench sources, $n=\\kver_{\\text{drop}}/(\\kver/n)_{\\text{drop}}$; this yields 135,622, 3,355, and 2,400 to within floating-point error. For NBA, the season-mean drop fraction $\\tfrac12(7/n_{14}+16/n_{15})=0.8756\\%$ and flip fraction $\\tfrac12(4/n_{14}+8/n_{15})=0.4567\\%$ form a $2\\times2$ linear system in $(1/n_{14},1/n_{15})$. Its solution gives $n_{14}=1{,}319$ and $n_{15}=1{,}311$. The two seasons sum to 2,630 games; we could not check this against the harness's season file, which is not among our artifacts. The script \\nolinkurl{analysis/derive_stats.py} performs this derivation from \\texttt{paper\\_data.json} and writes \\nolinkurl{analysis/derived_stats.json}.\n336:This subsection records the evaluation harness's stated protocol. The harness source is not among the artifacts accompanying this paper, so these details are reported as specified and could not be inspected. Only the statements about what the finder receives are confirmed by the finder's own interface documentation (\\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 1--46).\n360:Three refined search configurations were designed to target that improvement. As recorded, they are relinearized category-knapsack variants: one with multi-pair targeting and pruning, one with swap refinement, and one with pruning seeded from the baseline influence set. No source for these configurations is among our artifacts, so we give no further implementation detail. All three were unscored (0 of 3 scored), and the evaluation evidence does not record why. Their effect on $\\kver$ is unknown and should not be read as a loss. No result in the main text depends on them.\n368:All data are public. The snapshots below are those listed in the task's data specification; that the harness loaded exactly these files, and the split it applied, could not be verified from our artifacts. Arena: \\nolinkurl{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-140k} at commit \\texttt{6322995ab34d}, CC-BY-4.0. MT-Bench: \\nolinkurl{lmsys/mt_bench_human_judgments} at commit \\texttt{f7d2896d2cc5}, human and GPT-4-pair files, CC-BY-4.0. NBA: \\nolinkurl{fivethirtyeight/data} at commit \\texttt{4c1ff5e3aef1}, file \\nolinkurl{nba-elo/nbaallelo.csv}, CC-BY-4.0. Development used the Arena 55k release (commit \\texttt{18c298340948}) and the NBA 1983 season.\n371:Python, CPU only; the finder depends only on NumPy and the standard library. The full sealed evaluation runs the 17 cases under three seeds plus one replay. Recorded episode time was about 16\\,s per seed; the evaluator's comparator runs add to this.\n373:\\paragraph{Artifacts.}\n376:\\subsection{LLM usage}\n379:Large language models were used extensively and are disclosed here. An autonomous LLM-based research agent proposed the study design within the task's constraints. It implemented the perturbation finder and the refined configurations, wrote the analysis and plotting scripts, and drafted this manuscript, including its framing and text. Every number in the paper comes from the sealed evaluation record produced by executing the code, and the derivation scripts are included. Bibliographic metadata was retrieved from the Semantic Scholar API. Huang et al.~(2025) is cited by title only, as part of the provided background reading list. No result or method in this paper is taken from it. No paid API or GPU was used for the experiments.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
