{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FH8oN2HkxiKVqNyQzJyZoY","type":"tool_result","content":"\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n(i) A refit-verified, influence-ordered protocol for leaderboard fragility. It covers two perturbation types (dropping and flipping comparisons) and two targets (top-1 and top-$k$ set). It is checked against a planted case with an enumerated minimum, a row-permutation control, and seed replay (\\S\\ref{sec:method}, \\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}).\n(ii) A cross-design measurement on pinned public snapshots of four evaluation designs, with every size reported both as a count and as a fraction of the data (\\S\\ref{sec:res-main}).\n(iii) Three regularities with stated limits. Flipping always needs fewer comparisons than dropping. The top-$k$ boundary can be far cheaper or dearer to move than the leader. Fragility rankings across designs reverse between the count and fraction scales (\\S\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}).\n(iv) An explicit negative result: the search procedure we evaluate does not improve on the plain influence ordering. The pre-declared test of that improvement is unsupported, so we make no algorithmic-superiority claim (\\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}).\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Protocol overview. Comparison rows from four evaluation designs are fit with a ridge Bradley--Terry model. Rows are ordered by their one-step influence on the decisive margin: leader vs.\\ runner-up for top-1, or the $k$-th vs.\\ $(k{+}1)$-th player for a top-$k$ set. The shortest prefix whose removal or outcome reversal changes the target is found by exponential and binary search. Every probe is an exact refit, and the final set is re-verified independently. Sizes are reported as a count $\\kver$ and as a fraction $\\kver/n$. A random-subset comparator, a direct leader--challenger heuristic, a planted-minimum control, a row-permutation control, and seed replay calibrate the instrument.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\n\\paragraph{Artifacts.}\nPerturbation finder: \\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py} (identical to the baseline tree); BT refit at lines 72--117, influence ordering and verified prefix search at lines 151--217. The evaluation harness is not included (\\S\\ref{sec:setup}). Derived statistics, Table~\\ref{tab:cases}, and Table~\\ref{tab:percase}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/derive_stats.py}. Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:contrasts}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/build_figures.py}, which reads only \\nolinkurl{analysis/derived_stats.json}. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e6af20b8-3edd-4696-ac58-f407db387098","uuid":"4e511d1c-76be-4978-a474-3a9ce678a5e4","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:41:50.724Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n(i) A refit-verified, influence-ordered protocol for leaderboard fragility. It covers two perturbation types (dropping and flipping comparisons) and two targets (top-1 and top-$k$ set). It is checked against a planted case with an enumerated minimum, a row-permutation control, and seed replay (\\S\\ref{sec:method}, \\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}).\n(ii) A cross-design measurement on pinned public snapshots of four evaluation designs, with every size reported both as a count and as a fraction of the data (\\S\\ref{sec:res-main}).\n(iii) Three regularities with stated limits. Flipping always needs fewer comparisons than dropping. The top-$k$ boundary can be far cheaper or dearer to move than the leader. Fragility rankings across designs reverse between the count and fraction scales (\\S\\ref{sec:res-contrasts}).\n(iv) An explicit negative result: the search procedure we evaluate does not improve on the plain influence ordering. The pre-declared test of that improvement is unsupported, so we make no algorithmic-superiority claim (\\S\\ref{sec:res-validity}).\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Protocol overview. Comparison rows from four evaluation designs are fit with a ridge Bradley--Terry model. Rows are ordered by their one-step influence on the decisive margin: leader vs.\\ runner-up for top-1, or the $k$-th vs.\\ $(k{+}1)$-th player for a top-$k$ set. The shortest prefix whose removal or outcome reversal changes the target is found by exponential and binary search. Every probe is an exact refit, and the final set is re-verified independently. Sizes are reported as a count $\\kver$ and as a fraction $\\kver/n$. A random-subset comparator, a direct leader--challenger heuristic, a planted-minimum control, a row-permutation control, and seed replay calibrate the instrument.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\n\\paragraph{Artifacts.}\nPerturbation finder: \\nolinkurl{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py} (identical to the baseline tree); BT refit at lines 72--117, influence ordering and verified prefix search at lines 151--217. The evaluation harness is not included (\\S\\ref{sec:setup}). Derived statistics, Table~\\ref{tab:cases}, and Table~\\ref{tab:percase}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/derive_stats.py}. Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:contrasts}: \\nolinkurl{analysis/build_figures.py}, which reads only \\nolinkurl{analysis/derived_stats.json}. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
